    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d421-a2">

                <pb facs="0757" n="661" xml:id="pg661"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 43.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bingham to Mr. Hawes.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation, Japan</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Yokohama</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-01-14"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 14,
                        1874</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 45.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your No. 288, of date the 6th
                    instant, I have to say that the general original jurisdiction in all civil and
                    criminal cases against the citizens of the United States in Japan under the
                    treaty of 1858, and the laws of the United States, is vested in the United
                    States consuls; and that, by the twenty-seventh section of the act of June 22,
                    1860, I can have appellate jurisdiction only of cases like that presented in
                    your letter. As no appeal has been taken from your judgment to me in the case of
                    Mr. Wood, it does not seem proper that I should express any opinion on the
                    subject.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JNO. A. BINGHAM.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
